.Dd $Mdocdate: November 2 2026 $
.Dt DCARPD 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm dcarpd
.Nd Daemon implementing DCARP protocol
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl dnv
.Op Fl f Ar state_file
.Op Fl g Ar backup_gateway
.Fl i Ar interface
.Op Fl k Ar key_file
.Op Fl s Ar sync_interface
.Op Fl x Ar program
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
daemon implements the DCARP protocol for dynamic IP address sharing.
It gives the address of a
.Xr carp 4
interface, which the master may get from DHCP,
to the backups,
and writes it with the gateway of that network to a state file,
from which
.Xr ifstated 8
or other scripts set the routes.
.Nm
changes no routes itself.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl d
Do not daemonize.
If this option is specified,
.Nm
will run in the foreground and log to standard error.
.It Fl f Ar state_file
Write the configuration held to
.Ar state_file
instead of
.Pa /var/db/dcarpd/ Ns Ar interface .
.It Fl g Ar backup_gateway
This host's address on another network that it shares with the other
members, typically an internal network.
As master it announces it,
and the other members use it as their gateway while they are backup and
cannot reach the gateway of the
.Fl i
interface's network themselves.
.It Fl i Ar interface
The
.Xr carp 4
interface whose address
.Nm
manages.
.It Fl k Ar key_file
Read the shared key from
.Ar key_file
instead of
.Pa /etc/dcarp.key .
The file must be owned by root and not readable by group or others.
.It Fl n
Run in dry-run mode.
In this mode, the daemon goes through all the motions but does not
actually reconfigure network interfaces.
This is useful for testing.
.It Fl s Ar sync_interface
Send and receive the DCARP messages on
.Ar sync_interface ,
for example a dedicated link between the nodes,
instead of on the
.Fl i
interface.
Messages received on any other interface are rejected.
.It Fl v
Increase verbosity.
Can be specified multiple times.
.It Fl x Ar program
Run
.Ar program ,
an absolute path,
whenever the state file is written and whenever the
.Xr carp 4
state of the
.Fl i
interface changes,
with the state file and the state,
.Cm MASTER ,
.Cm BACKUP
or
.Cm INIT ,
as arguments,
for example to set the routes.
.Nm
does not wait for it,
but runs one at a time:
a run asked for while one is running is started when that one exits,
and then gets the state of that time.
A
.Ar program
that exits with a status other than 0 is logged.
.El
.Pp
As master,
.Nm
sends the address and netmask of the
.Fl i
interface,
the gateway of that network,
which it takes from its default route if that goes through the
.Fl i
interface, such as one
.Xr dhcpleased 8
adds from the lease,
and its
.Fl g
backup gateway.
A backup that has become master may still route via the old master;
until it has a default route of its own through the
.Fl i
interface it sends the gateway it last took or sent,
if that is on the interface's network.
It sends them when it becomes master,
when that address changes,
and every 10 seconds.
.Pp
Each configuration has a generation:
the modification time of the DHCP lease
.Pa /var/db/dhcpleased/ Ns Ar interface ,
or 0 without one,
and the time
.Nm
sees the address of the
.Fl i
interface change otherwise.
In any state
.Nm
takes a configuration of a newer generation than its own,
and rejects and logs one of an older generation.
So a backup that has become master because the master's address
changed, and with it the
.Xr carp 4
authentication, takes the new address and returns to backup,
and the master does not take the old address back from it.
.Pp
Of the same generation,
a backup takes the configuration of any sender:
only a master sends, and
.Xr carp 4
has elected it,
even one of worse priority than the backup's, as after the backup
comes back with
.Va net.inet.carp.preempt
disabled.
So does a member in the INIT state, which sends nothing,
for example one whose
.Fl i
interface has no address yet.
As master,
.Nm
takes it only from a sender with a better
.Xr carp 4
priority, that is a lower demote count, or else a lower advbase and
advskew,
and of the same priority from the sender with the lower sender id,
a random number each
.Nm
chooses when it starts.
This decides between members that are master at the same time,
and see each other's messages but not each other's
.Xr carp 4
advertisements.
It rejects and logs any other.
.Pp
To take a configuration,
.Nm
makes its address the only IPv4 address of the
.Fl i
interface and writes it to the state file,
but only when it differs from the one it took last.
As master it writes the configuration it sends,
but keeps the backup gateway it learned from the member that was master
before, which it needs when it becomes backup again;
its own is no use to it.
The state file consists of
.Xr sh 1
variable assignments, replaced atomically:
.Bl -tag -width DCARP_GENERATION
.It Ev DCARP_INTERFACE
The
.Fl i
interface.
.It Ev DCARP_ADDRESS
Its address.
.It Ev DCARP_NETMASK
Its netmask.
.It Ev DCARP_GATEWAY
The gateway on its network, to use as master, or empty if there is none.
.It Ev DCARP_BACKUP_GATEWAY
The gateway to use as backup: the
.Fl g
address of the member that was master last,
or empty if there is none.
.It Ev DCARP_GENERATION
The generation, in seconds since the Epoch.
.El
.Pp
A backup thus knows the gateway the moment it becomes master,
and a master the gateway to use the moment it becomes backup.
.Pp
Messages are encrypted and authenticated with XChaCha20-Poly1305,
keyed with the SHA-256 hash of the key file.
At startup and when the key file is reread on
.Dv SIGHUP ,
.Nm
logs a fingerprint of the key,
which must be the same on all nodes.
.Pp
A message is accepted only if it authenticates,
it is at most 30 seconds old,
and its sequence number is higher than the last one accepted from the
same sender.
The last 64 senders are remembered,
more than the members of any group.
Rejected messages are logged with their source address,
at most once every 10 seconds.
The clocks of the nodes must therefore agree within 30 seconds.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/etc/examples/ifstated-dcarpd.conf" -compact
.It Pa /etc/dcarp.key
The shared key.
.It Pa /etc/examples/ifstated-dcarpd.conf
Example
.Xr ifstated.conf 5
for a pair of firewalls.
.It Pa /var/db/dcarpd/ Ns Ar interface
The state file.
.It Pa /var/db/dhcpleased/ Ns Ar interface
The DHCP lease of the carp interface, whose time is the generation.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Run
.Nm
on both firewalls of a pair, for carp0, whose address the master gets
by DHCP, with the firewall's own address on the internal network:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# rcctl enable dcarpd
# rcctl set dcarpd flags -i carp0 -g 192.168.1.2
# rcctl start dcarpd
.Ed
.Pp
In
.Xr ifstated.conf 5 ,
route via the gateways from the state file when carp0 changes state,
and keep the current route if there is none:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
if carp0.link.up {
	run "[ -r /var/db/dcarpd/carp0 ] && . /var/db/dcarpd/carp0 && \e
	    [ -n \e"$DCARP_GATEWAY\e" ] && \e
	    { route -q delete default; \e
	    route -q add default $DCARP_GATEWAY; }"
}
if carp0.link.down {
	run "[ -r /var/db/dcarpd/carp0 ] && . /var/db/dcarpd/carp0 && \e
	    [ -n \e"$DCARP_BACKUP_GATEWAY\e" ] && \e
	    { route -q delete default; \e
	    route -q add default $DCARP_BACKUP_GATEWAY; }"
}
.Ed
.Pp
A complete example, which also demotes the master if the gateway is
unreachable, is in
.Pa /etc/examples/ifstated-dcarpd.conf .
.Pp
With more than two members a backup must route via a new master
although it stays backup, and its carp0 does not change state.
Leave the routes out of
.Xr ifstated.conf 5
then, and set them with a program run by
.Fl x ,
here
.Pa /etc/dcarpd-route :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#!/bin/sh
\&. "$1" || exit 1
case $2 in
MASTER)	gw=$DCARP_GATEWAY ;;
BACKUP)	gw=$DCARP_BACKUP_GATEWAY ;;
*)	exit 0 ;;
esac
[ -n "$gw" ] || exit 0
route -q change default $gw 2>/dev/null || route -q add default $gw
.Ed
.Pp
and on each member, with its own internal address:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# rcctl set dcarpd flags -i carp0 -g 192.168.1.2 -x /etc/dcarpd-route
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr carp 4 ,
.Xr ifstated.conf 5 ,
.Xr dhcpleased 8 ,
.Xr ifstated 8 ,
.Xr syslogd 8
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
command first appeared in
.Ox 7.4 .
.Sh AUTHORS
.An -nosplit
The
.Nm
program was written by
.An Niklas Hallqvist .
